rules:
- compliance:
    enabled: true
    validation_type: quality
  id: rule-decision-schema-must-be-structured
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architecture decisions must be expressed using a structured YAML decision
    schema (ADR) rather than unstructured narrative text to enable machine processing
    and consistency.
  title: Rule Decision Schema Must Be Structured
- compliance:
    enabled: true
    validation_type: governance
  id: rule-adr-single-source-of-truth
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture decisions must be recorded and updated only in decision records
    (ADR); all other representations, such as maps and documents, must be derived
    from ADR.
  title: Rule Adr Single Source Of Truth
- id: rule-patterns-born-as-supporting
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All patterns must be created as supporting by default and never directly
    as core to ensure validation through evidence and usage before becoming foundational.
  title: Rule Patterns Born As Supporting
- id: rule-do-not-merge-across-architectural-layers
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Patterns must not be merged or consolidated across different architectural
    layers (e.g., governance, execution, contracts) to preserve architectural clarity.
  title: Rule Do Not Merge Across Architectural Layers
- id: rule-precedence-hierarchy
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Defines the precedence model for resolving conflicts: Global Rules > Project
    Rules > Prompt Rules. Global rules define hard constraints that cannot be overridden.'
  title: Rule Precedence Hierarchy
- id: rule-heuristic-patterns-require-signals
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All heuristic-derived patterns must include at least one linked signal
    as evidence to ensure traceability and grounding in observed behavior.
  title: Rule Heuristic Patterns Require Signals
- compliance:
    enabled: true
    validation_type: governance
  id: rule-pattern-decision-lifecycle-alignment
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Distinguishes between pattern maturity (knowledge evolution) and decision
    status (governance lifecycle), ensuring consistent interpretation across the system.
  title: Rule Pattern Decision Lifecycle Alignment
- id: rule-signal-promotion-criteria
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Candidate signals must satisfy mandatory recurrence, system impact, reusability,
    and stability criteria before being promoted to patterns.
  title: Rule Signal Promotion Criteria
- id: rule-workflow-execution-model-compliance
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'All runtime-native workflows must comply with the CAS separation of concerns:
    Runner orchestrates, Workflows reason, and Providers supply knowledge.'
  title: Rule Workflow Execution Model Compliance
- id: rule-decision-status-drives-stage
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: The lifecycle stage of any architecture item must be derived from its decision
    status; items not marked 'accepted' cannot be classified as 'DONE'.
  title: Rule Decision Status Drives Stage
- id: rule-aem-consistency-with-decisions
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: The Architecture Evolution Map (AEM) must reflect and never contradict
    the decisions recorded in the Architecture Decision Records (ADR).
  title: Rule Aem Consistency With Decisions
- id: rule-system-proposes-architect-decides
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be proposed by the system and explicitly
    accepted, rejected, or deferred by a human architect to maintain control.
  title: Rule System Proposes Architect Decides
- id: rule-decisions-must-be-persisted
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be persisted as structured ADRs before
    being reflected in the Architecture Evolution Map (AEM) or other system artifacts.
  title: Rule Decisions Must Be Persisted
- id: rule-core-patterns-must-be-foundational
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Core patterns must represent foundational architectural primitives rather
    than specific strategies or implementations to maintain long-term system stability.
  title: Rule Core Patterns Must Be Foundational
- id: rule-promotion-requires-evidence
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Pattern promotion to core must be supported by strong evidence, ideally
    from multiple sources or across multiple system instances.
  title: Rule Promotion Requires Evidence
- id: rule-language-semantic-contract
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Defines the semantic contract for architecture language, distinguishing
    between intent (AID), state (AEM), and governance-approved decisions (ADR).
  title: Rule Language Semantic Contract
summary:
  global_rules: 16
  project_rules: 0
  total_rules: 16
